{
  "id": "k-closest-points-to-origin",
  "questionId": "k-closest-points-to-origin",
  "slug": "k-closest-points-to-origin",
  "title": "K Closest Points to Origin",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Heap",
  "patterns": [
    "heap",
    "top k",
    "max-heap",
    "geometry"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "sorting every point by distance does more work than necessary when k is much smaller than n",
    "you only need to track the k closest points seen so far - can a bounded heap help you discard the rest early?",
    "use a max-heap of size k keyed on squared distance; the heap's top is always the farthest of your current k candidates",
    "push each point's (distance, point) onto the heap; whenever the heap exceeds size k, pop the max (farthest). what's left at the end is your k closest, which you then sort for the deterministic tie-break order"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "computing actual Euclidean distance with a square root instead of comparing squared distances directly",
    "using a min-heap over all n points and popping k times, which is correct but strictly less efficient than a bounded max-heap of size k",
    "not applying a deterministic secondary sort when distances tie, making output order inconsistent"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of points on a 2D plane, each written as `[x, y]`,\nand a number `k`. Distance is measured using squared Euclidean distance\n(x*x + y*y) so that everything stays an integer, no square roots\nneeded.\n\n**Return the `k` points closest to the origin (0, 0), ordered by\ndistance ascending.**\n\nIf two points tie on distance, break the tie by x ascending, and if\nthat also ties, by y ascending, so the output is fully deterministic.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= k <= points.length <= 10^4",
      "-10^4 <= xi, yi <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "k equal to the total number of points, meaning every point is returned",
      "multiple points tied at the exact same distance",
      "a point located exactly at the origin",
      "single point in the list"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "points = [[1, 3], [-2, 2]], k = 1",
        "output": "[[-2, 2]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[-2, 2]]",
        "explanation": "distance of (1,3) is 1+9=10; distance of (-2,2) is 4+4=8, which is smaller"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "points = [[3, 3], [5, -1], [-2, 4]], k = 2",
        "output": "[[3, 3], [-2, 4]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[3, 3], [-2, 4]]",
        "explanation": "distances are 18, 26, and 20 respectively; the two smallest are (3,3) at 18 and (-2,4) at 20"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "points = [[0, 1], [1, 0]], k = 2",
        "output": "[[0, 1], [1, 0]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[0, 1], [1, 0]]",
        "explanation": "both points have distance 1, a tie; (0,1) comes first because its x value (0) is smaller than (1,0)'s x value (1)"
      }
    ],
